= Paper 2
{scope}

https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/undergradnst/files/2002/PaperNST_IB_2.pdf[Original examination paper]. The solutions below cover all ten questions, including every lettered and numbered part. The original PDF is authoritative for notation and matrices.

= 1
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Apply the <Laplacian> in <polar coordinates> twice to the proposed separated function. For any real exponent $m$,
$$
\nabla^2(r^m\cos2\theta)=(m^2-4)r^{m-2}\cos2\theta,
$$
so the <biharmonic equation> becomes
$$
\nabla^4(r^m\cos2\theta)=(m^2-4)((m-2)^2-4)r^{m-4}\cos2\theta=0.
$$
Its four distinct roots are $m=-2,0,2,4$. The <separated polar solutions of the biharmonic equation> therefore give the complete radial family in this angular sector,
$$
\psi=(Ar^{-2}+B+Cr^2+Dr^4)\cos2\theta.
$$
Equivalently, its radial equation is a fourth-order <Cauchy-Euler equation>; four distinct powers supply four <linearly independent> solutions, so no logarithmic terms are required.

**Interior.** Continuity with value zero at the origin removes both $r^{-2}\cos2\theta$ and $\cos2\theta$. The latter depends on the approach direction even though it is bounded. The two <boundary conditions> at unit radius then give $C+D=0$ and $2C+4D=1$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\psi_{\mathrm{in}}(r,\theta)=\tfrac12(r^4-r^2)\cos2\theta,\qquad 0\leq r\leq1.}
$$
This extends smoothly through the origin: $r^2\cos2\theta=x^2-y^2$ and $r^4\cos2\theta=(x^2+y^2)(x^2-y^2)$ are <polynomials>. Its radial <derivative> at $r=1$ is $(2r^3-r)\cos2\theta=\cos2\theta$.

**Exterior.** The condition on the radial <derivative> at infinity excludes the $r^2$ and $r^4$ terms. A nonzero $B\cos2\theta$ is permitted, since the required decay concerns the radial <derivative>. Now $A+B=0$ and $-2A=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(r,\theta)=\tfrac12(1-r^{-2})\cos2\theta,\qquad r\geq1.}
$$
Indeed $\partial_r\psi_{\mathrm{out}}=r^{-3}\cos2\theta\to0$, and its value at unit radius is zero. These are particular interior and exterior solutions for the <biharmonic streamfunction>; no condition requiring $\psi$ itself to vanish at infinity was imposed.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

**Integral identity and representation.** Define $L=\nabla^2+k^2$ and take $n$ to be the outward unit normal. The <product rule> gives
$$
\nabla\cdot(\phi\nabla\psi-\psi\nabla\phi)=\phi\nabla^2\psi-\psi\nabla^2\phi=\phi L\psi-\psi L\phi.
$$
The $k^2\phi\psi$ terms cancel. Integrating and applying the <divergence theorem> proves the <Helmholtz equation> version of <Green's second identity>:
$$
\boxed{\int_V(\phi L\psi-\psi L\phi)\,dV=\int_S(\phi\partial_n\psi-\psi\partial_n\phi)\,dS.}
$$

Choose the <Dirichlet Green function> with the sign convention
$$
L_xG(x,x')=\delta(x-x'),\qquad G(x,x')=0\quad(x\in S),
$$
where $\delta$ is the three-dimensional <Dirac delta>. Substitute $\psi=G$, use $L\phi=f$, and evaluate the <Dirac delta> integral. The boundary term containing $G$ vanishes and $\phi=g$ on $S$, leaving
$$
\phi(x')-\int_VG(x,x')f(x)\,dV=\int_Sg(x)\partial_nG(x,x')\,dS.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\phi(x')=\int_VG(x,x')f(x)\,dV+\int_Sg(x)\partial_nG(x,x')\,dS.}
$$
The choice of sign in the <Green function> equation matters: it fixes both the source normalization and these signs. For a bounded region this construction presumes that the chosen $k^2$ is not a <Dirichlet eigenvalue> of $-\nabla^2$; at such an eigenvalue a Green inverse for arbitrary data does not exist.

**Infinite space.** Put $r=|x-x'|$. Away from the source, spherical symmetry reduces the <Helmholtz equation> to
$$
G''+\frac2rG'+k^2G=0,\qquad (rG)''+k^2(rG)=0.
$$
For $k\ne0$ its general radial solution is
$$
G(r)=\frac{A\cos kr+B\sin kr}{r}.
$$
Integrate $LG=\delta$ over a ball of radius $\varepsilon$ about the source. The <divergence theorem> yields
$$
4\pi\varepsilon^2G'(\varepsilon)+k^2\int_{r<\varepsilon}G\,dV=1.
$$
Since $G=A/r+Bk+O(r)$, the volume term is $O(\varepsilon^2)$ and the flux tends to $-4\pi A$. Therefore $A=-1/(4\pi)$, while $B$ remains free:
$$
\boxed{G(r)=-\frac{\cos kr}{4\pi r}+B\frac{\sin kr}{r},\qquad r>0.}
$$
The $B$ term extends regularly through the source and solves the homogeneous <Helmholtz equation>, so it cannot change the unit <Dirac delta> normalization. A condition at infinity is needed to select it. For example, with time dependence $e^{-i\omega t}$, the <outgoing Green function for the three-dimensional Helmholtz equation> is $-e^{ikr}/(4\pi r)$, corresponding to $B=-i/(4\pi)$.

**Zero wavenumber.** For fixed finite $B$, $\sin(kr)/r\to0$ as $k\to0$, and
$$
\boxed{G(r)\longrightarrow-\frac1{4\pi r},\qquad\nabla^2G=\delta.}
$$
This is the decaying <Green function of the Laplacian>. Directly at $k=0$, the general radial solution is $A/r+C$; source normalization fixes $A=-1/(4\pi)$, and decay at infinity removes $C$. If the free parameter depends on $k$ as $B(k)\sim C/k$, the limit instead includes that arbitrary homogeneous constant. Thus the usual Laplace limit holds for a fixed parameter, or after a suitable condition at infinity has been selected.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

**Branches and cuts.** For the <square root of z squared minus one>, a <branch point> is a point around which <analytic continuation> along an arbitrarily small closed loop can change the value of a chosen <branch of a multivalued function>. Near $z=1$, the square root behaves as $\sqrt2(z-1)^{1/2}$; near $z=-1$ it is a nonzero analytic factor times $(z+1)^{1/2}$. One circuit around either point reverses its sign. Thus the finite <branch points> are $\boxed{z=\pm1}$. Infinity is not a <branch point>: in $w=1/z$ the expression is $w^{-1}\sqrt{1-w^2}$, which has two single-valued meromorphic choices near zero.

A single <branch cut> can join $-1$ to $1$; its shortest choice is the real segment $[-1,1]$. Any surviving loop then surrounds either both <branch points> or neither, so it produces no sign change. Alternatively, use two <branch cuts>, $(-\infty,-1]$ and $[1,\infty)$, each joining a <branch point> to infinity. On the resulting slit region one can choose a consistent <branch of a multivalued function>.

For the finite cut, fix $f(z)\sim z$ at infinity, equivalently $f(x)>0$ for $x>1$. To give its actual values on both sides, put
$$
a=x^2-y^2-1,\qquad \rho=\sqrt{a^2+4x^2y^2},\qquad U=\sqrt{\frac{\rho+a}{2}},\quad V=\sqrt{\frac{\rho-a}{2}}.
$$
For $y\ne0$, the selected <square root> is
$$
\boxed{f(x+iy)=\operatorname{sgn}(x)U+i\operatorname{sgn}(y)V.}
$$
Here $\operatorname{sgn}(0)=0$; when $x=0$, $U=0$. Squaring verifies the formula because $UV=|xy|$. Its real part has the sign of $x$, its imaginary part has the sign of $y$, and it matches the chosen value at infinity. In particular, the two boundary values on the <branch cut> are
$$
\boxed{f(x+i0)=+i\sqrt{1-x^2},\qquad f(x-i0)=-i\sqrt{1-x^2},\quad -1<x<1.}
$$
Outside the cut, $f(x)=\operatorname{sgn}(x)\sqrt{x^2-1}$. Reversing the global <branch of a multivalued function> reverses every displayed value.

**Differential equation.** Write $f(\zeta)=\sqrt{\zeta^2-1}$ with this finite cut, and orient $C$ counterclockwise with one circuit around it. Since $C$ is fixed and avoids the <branch points>, <differentiation under the integral sign> gives
$$
u'(z)=\oint_C\frac{\zeta e^{z\zeta}}{f(\zeta)}\,d\zeta,\qquad u''(z)=\oint_C\frac{\zeta^2e^{z\zeta}}{f(\zeta)}\,d\zeta.
$$
On the contour $f'=\zeta/f$. Therefore
$$
zu''+u'-zu=\oint_Ce^{z\zeta}\left(zf(\zeta)+\frac{\zeta}{f(\zeta)}\right)d\zeta=\oint_C\frac d{d\zeta}\left[e^{z\zeta}f(\zeta)\right]d\zeta=0.
$$
The last <contour integral> vanishes because the expression in brackets returns to its original value after circling both <branch points>. This proves $\boxed{zu''+u'-zu=0}$, including at $z=0$; division by $z$ is unnecessary.

\Image[paper-2-finite-branch-cut.png]
{title=Shrinking a counterclockwise contour around the finite square-root cut}
{description=The upper lip runs from 1 to -1, and the lower lip returns from -1 to 1. The endpoint arcs have vanishing integrals. The values shown use the branch that behaves as z at infinity.}

**Value at zero.** Near either endpoint $\zeta_0=\pm1$, the integrand at zero is $O(|\zeta-\zeta_0|^{-1/2})$. A small arc of radius $\varepsilon$ has length $O(\varepsilon)$ and hence contributes $O(\sqrt\varepsilon)\to0$. The upper lip runs from $1$ to $-1$, and the lower lip from $-1$ to $1$. Thus
$$
u(0)=\int_1^{-1}\frac{dx}{i\sqrt{1-x^2}}+\int_{-1}^1\frac{dx}{-i\sqrt{1-x^2}}=2i\int_{-1}^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}.
$$
Using $x=\sin t$ evaluates the final <integral> as $\pi$, and so
$$
\boxed{u(0)=2\pi i.}
$$
The same deformation gives $u(z)=2i\int_{-1}^1e^{zx}(1-x^2)^{-1/2}dx$. The sign of $u(0)$ reverses if either the global square-root choice or the contour orientation is reversed; the differential equation is unaffected.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

**Definitions.** In a punctured neighborhood of $z_0$, let the <Laurent series> be $f(z)=\sum_{j=-\infty}^{\infty}c_j(z-z_0)^j$. The point is a <pole> of order $m\geq1$ if $c_{-m}\ne0$ and every coefficient with index less than $-m$ vanishes. Its <residue> is $\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z_0}f=c_{-1}}$. A <simple pole> has $m=1$.

**Poles for $0<a<1$.** Denote the bracket in the denominator by $D(z)$. Factoring gives
$$
D(z)=[(a-1)z^2+(a+1)][(a+1)z^2+(a-1)].
$$
Put $q=\sqrt{(1-a)/(1+a)}$, so $0<q<1$. The finite <poles> are $0,\pm q,\pm q^{-1}$, all simple; the numerator is nonzero at each. At zero,
$$
\operatorname{Res}_0f=\frac1{a^2-1}=-\frac1{1-a^2}.
$$
At a nonzero simple root $r$ of $D$, the <residue> formula gives
$$
\operatorname{Res}_rf=\frac{(r^2+1)^2}{rD'(r)},\qquad D'(r)=4r[a^2(r^2+1)-(r^2-1)].
$$
For $r^2=q^2$, $D'(r)=8ar$; for $r^2=q^{-2}$, $D'(r)=-8ar$. Substitution yields
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}\text{point}&\text{pole order}&\text{residue}\\\hline
0&1&-1/(1-a^2)\\
+q,-q&1&1/[2a(1-a^2)]\\
+q^{-1},-q^{-1}&1&-1/[2a(1-a^2)]
\end{array}\quad(0<a<1).}
$$
The two <zeros of a function> at $z=\pm i$ do not cause additional <poles>: there $D=-4$ and the numerator vanishes.

**The case $a=1$.** Simplify before using any formulas that divide by $1-a^2$:
$$
D(z)=(z^2+1)^2-(z^2-1)^2=4z^2,\qquad f(z)=\frac14\left(z+\frac2z+\frac1{z^3}\right).
$$
Thus its only finite <pole> is
$$
\boxed{z=0:\quad\text{order }3,\qquad\operatorname{Res}_0f=\tfrac12\quad(a=1).}
$$
As $a\uparrow1$, the two inner <poles> merge with zero while the outer two escape to infinity. If one also considers the <Riemann sphere>, $f$ at $a=1$ has a simple <pole> at infinity because $f(z)\sim z/4$; the requested unit-circle calculation only uses finite <poles>.

**Unit-circle integral.** Set $z=e^{i\theta}$. Then
$$
\tan\theta=\frac{z^2-1}{i(z^2+1)},\qquad d\theta=\frac{dz}{iz},\qquad\frac{d\theta}{a^2+\tan^2\theta}=\frac1i f(z)\,dz.
$$
At the two angles where the tangent is undefined, the real integrand has continuous limiting value zero. Thus the substitution is valid for the <improper integral>, or after that continuous extension. No <pole> lies on $|z|=1$ for any allowed $a$.

For $0<a<1$, the sum of the enclosed <residues> is
$$
-\frac1{1-a^2}+\frac1{a(1-a^2)}=\frac1{a(1+a)}.
$$
The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{I=\frac1i\oint_{|z|=1}f(z)\,dz=\frac{2\pi}{a(1+a)},\qquad0<a\leq1.}
$$
At $a=1$ the enclosed <residue> is $1/2$, giving $I=\pi$, exactly the same formula. The direct simplification $1/(1+\tan^2\theta)=\cos^2\theta$ provides an independent check of that endpoint.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For a piecewise continuous function of exponential order on $t\geq0$, its unilateral <Laplace transform> is
$$
\boxed{F(p)=\int_0^\infty e^{-pt}f(t)\,dt.}
$$
The <Bromwich inversion formula>, at a point of continuity, is
$$
\boxed{f(t)=\frac1{2\pi i}\lim_{T\to\infty}\int_{\gamma-iT}^{\gamma+iT}e^{pt}F(p)\,dp.}
$$
The <Bromwich contour> is a straight vertical line, traversed upwards, with $\gamma$ to the right of the abscissa of convergence and hence of all singularities of the transform. Under the usual piecewise smoothness assumptions, at a jump the symmetric inversion limit gives the mean of the two one-sided values.

In the transform in the question, put $\lambda=e^{-t}$. Then $d\lambda=-e^{-t}dt$ and
$$
H(s)=\int_0^\infty e^{-st}h(e^{-t})\,dt.
$$
Thus the <restricted Mellin transform on the unit interval> is the <Laplace transform> of $f(t)=h(e^{-t})$. Apply the <Bromwich inversion formula> with $t=-\log\lambda$, where $0<\lambda<1$, to obtain
$$
\boxed{h(\lambda)=\frac1{2\pi i}\lim_{T\to\infty}\int_{\gamma-iT}^{\gamma+iT}\lambda^{-s}H(s)\,ds.}
$$
Here $\gamma$ lies in the convergence half-plane of $H$. At a discontinuity this formula has the same mean-value interpretation. The real <natural logarithm> defines $\lambda^{-s}$ unambiguously.

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose $\gamma>1/2$. The inverse integrand is $\lambda^{-s}/[2(s-1/2)]$. Because $-\log\lambda>0$, the exponential factor decays towards the left. Closing the <Bromwich contour> to the left and using the <residue theorem> picks up just the <simple pole> at $s=1/2$, with <residue> $\lambda^{-1/2}/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{h(\lambda)=\frac1{2\sqrt\lambda},\qquad0<\lambda<1.}
$$
Its defining <integral> checks both the answer and its convergence condition:
$$
\int_0^1\lambda^{s-1}\frac1{2\sqrt\lambda}\,d\lambda=\frac1{2(s-1/2)}=\frac1{2s-1},\qquad\operatorname{Re}s>\tfrac12.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Now the inverse integrand can be written
$$
\lambda^{-s}H(s)=\frac{e^{s\log(1/(2\lambda))}}{2(s+1)},\qquad\gamma>-1.
$$
For $0<\lambda<1/2$, the exponent has a positive real time parameter. Close the <Bromwich contour> to the left; the <residue> at $s=-1$ is $\tfrac12(2\lambda)=\lambda$. For $1/2<\lambda<1$, close to the right, where there are no <poles>. The reversed orientation there still gives zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{h(\lambda)=\begin{cases}\lambda,&0<\lambda<\tfrac12,\\0,&\tfrac12<\lambda<1.\end{cases}}
$$
The same result follows from the <Laplace transform time-shift rule>: $H(s)=\tfrac12e^{-s\log2}/(s+1)$ corresponds to $f(t)=e^{-t}\Theta(t-\log2)$, where $\Theta$ is the <Heaviside step function>.

The original transform cannot fix a value at the single point $\lambda=1/2$. The <Bromwich inversion formula> chooses the average of the limiting values $1/2$ and zero, giving $\boxed{h(1/2)=1/4}$ under that convention. Indeed,
$$
\int_0^1\lambda^{s-1}h(\lambda)\,d\lambda=\int_0^{1/2}\lambda^s\,d\lambda=\frac{2^{-s-1}}{s+1},\qquad\operatorname{Re}s>-1,
$$
regardless of the assigned value at the jump.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <Cartesian tensors>, let $R$ be the <orthogonal matrix> taking old vector components to new ones, $v'_i=R_{ij}v_j$. A rank-$n$ <tensor> transforms by one copy of $R$ on each index:
$$
\boxed{T'_{i_1\cdots i_n}=R_{i_1j_1}\cdots R_{i_nj_n}T_{j_1\cdots j_n}.}
$$
Repeated indices are summed according to <Einstein notation>. In general coordinates a <contravariant tensor> index instead carries the corresponding <Jacobian matrix> $\partial x'^i/\partial x^j$, and a <covariant tensor> index carries its inverse; in <orthonormal bases> of Cartesian axes their component laws coincide with the displayed form.

An <isotropic tensor> has identical components after every proper <orthogonal transformation>, so $T'=T$ for every $R$ with $R^TR=I$ and $\det R=1$. Requiring reflection invariance as well is a stronger convention for odd tensor ranks. The <Kronecker delta> is invariant under all <orthogonal transformations>, while the <Levi-Civita symbol> is invariant under proper rotations and acquires a determinant sign under reflections.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

First separate the <symmetric tensor> and <antisymmetric tensor> parts:
$$
P_{ij}=\tfrac12(P_{ij}+P_{ji})+\tfrac12(P_{ij}-P_{ji}).
$$
The <trace> of the antisymmetric part vanishes. In three dimensions, remove one third of the trace from the symmetric part, and identify the remaining antisymmetric part with an <axial vector>. The <scalar, symmetric-traceless and axial tensor decomposition> is
$$
\boxed{P=\tfrac13P_{ii},\qquad S_{ij}=\tfrac12(P_{ij}+P_{ji})-\tfrac13P_{kk}\delta_{ij},\qquad A_k=\tfrac12\epsilon_{kij}P_{ij}.}
$$
Here $S_{ji}=S_{ij}$ and $S_{ii}=P_{ii}-P_{kk}=0$. The <contraction of two Levi-Civita symbols> verifies the reconstruction:
$$
\epsilon_{ijk}A_k=\tfrac12\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{k\ell m}P_{\ell m}=\tfrac12(\delta_{i\ell}\delta_{jm}-\delta_{im}\delta_{j\ell})P_{\ell m}=\tfrac12(P_{ij}-P_{ji}).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{P_{ij}=P\delta_{ij}+S_{ij}+\epsilon_{ijk}A_k.}
$$
The pieces contain $1$, $5$ and $3$ independent components respectively, accounting for all nine entries. Taking the trace, then the traceless symmetric part, then the antisymmetric part shows that the decomposition is unique.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Contract the given <isotropic fourth-rank tensor> with the <strain> array. The <stress tensor> is
$$
P_{ij}=\lambda\delta_{ij}p_{kk}+\mu p_{ij}+\nu p_{ji}.
$$
Write $p_{ij}=p\delta_{ij}+s_{ij}+\epsilon_{ijk}a_k$, where $s_{ij}$ is symmetric and traceless. Then $p_{kk}=3p$ and $p_{ji}=p\delta_{ij}+s_{ij}-\epsilon_{ijk}a_k$. Substitution gives
$$
P_{ij}=(3\lambda+\mu+\nu)p\delta_{ij}+(\mu+\nu)s_{ij}+(\mu-\nu)\epsilon_{ijk}a_k.
$$
By uniqueness of the <scalar, symmetric-traceless and axial tensor decomposition>,
$$
\boxed{P=(3\lambda+\mu+\nu)p,\qquad S_{ij}=(\mu+\nu)s_{ij},\qquad A_k=(\mu-\nu)a_k.}
$$
This is <isotropic elasticity on tensor components>: the three tensor sectors do not mix. If the <strain> is symmetric, $a_k=0$, hence $A_k=0$ and $\boxed{P_{ij}=P_{ji}}$. No equality between $\mu$ and $\nu$ is needed for that conclusion.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

A <normal mode> is a fixed displacement pattern whose components share a common harmonic time dependence in the equations for <small oscillations>. Its <normal frequency> is the associated oscillation frequency; below, $\omega$ denotes the angular frequency, so the frequency in cycles per unit time is $\omega/(2\pi)$. A <normal coordinate> is the amplitude of one such pattern, chosen so that the linear equations decouple. For <mass matrix> $M$ and <stiffness matrix> $K$, the patterns solve $Kv=\omega^2Mv$. Zero eigenvalues describe free rigid motions rather than periodic restoring oscillations.

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the equilibrium coordinates specified in the paper; their distances from the origin are one and their mutual separation is $\sqrt3$. Let $u_i=(x_i,y_i)$ be the small displacements. Each initially unstretched <spring> has, to first order, extension equal to the relative displacement projected along its original bond. Choose the three oriented extensions
$$
e_{12}=x_2-x_1,\qquad e_{13}=\tfrac12(x_3-x_1)+\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(y_3-y_1),\qquad e_{23}=-\tfrac12(x_3-x_2)+\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(y_3-y_2).
$$
The <kinetic energy> is exact in these displacement coordinates, while the <potential energy> needed for <small oscillations> is its quadratic part:
$$
\boxed{T=\frac m2\sum_{i=1}^3(\dot x_i^2+\dot y_i^2),\qquad V=\frac k2(e_{12}^2+e_{13}^2+e_{23}^2).}
$$
The factor $\sqrt3$ in the equilibrium bond length cancels when forming the unit bond directions. Applying the <Euler-Lagrange equation>, or differentiating $V$ for the force on each mass, gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
m\ddot x_1&=k(e_{12}+\tfrac12e_{13}),&m\ddot y_1&=\tfrac{\sqrt3 k}{2}e_{13},\\
m\ddot x_2&=-k(e_{12}+\tfrac12e_{23}),&m\ddot y_2&=\tfrac{\sqrt3 k}{2}e_{23},\\
m\ddot x_3&=-\tfrac k2(e_{13}-e_{23}),&m\ddot y_3&=-\tfrac{\sqrt3 k}{2}(e_{13}+e_{23}).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The total force vanishes componentwise, as expected for three masses coupled only by internal <spring> forces.

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Order coordinates as $q=(x_1,x_2,x_3;y_1,y_2,y_3)$. For either uniform displacement, $t_x=(1,1,1;0,0,0)$ or $t_y=(0,0,0;1,1,1)$, every relative displacement and every extension vanishes. These <normal modes> have zero <normal frequency> and represent two independent planar <translations>.

For $r=(-1,-1,2;\sqrt3,-\sqrt3,0)$, substitution gives
$$
e_{12}=0,\qquad e_{13}=\tfrac32-\tfrac32=0,\qquad e_{23}=-\tfrac32+\tfrac32=0.
$$
Thus $Kr=0$ too. A small rigid <rotation> through angle $\alpha$ moves equilibrium position $(X_i,Y_i)$ by $\alpha(-Y_i,X_i)$. With the specified positions this displacement is $-\alpha r/2$, identifying the third vector as infinitesimal rigid <rotation>. Hence
$$
\boxed{\omega^2(t_x)=\omega^2(t_y)=\omega^2(r)=0.}
$$
A zero-frequency <normal coordinate> obeys $\ddot Q=0$, so $Q=A+Bt$. This permits a constant displacement or a uniform velocity at the level of the linearized equations. In particular, the rotational pattern preserves lengths to first order; finite-angle rotation follows a curved path outside a single linear displacement pattern.

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <normal modes of an equilateral spring triangle>, the complement of the three rigid motions has dimension three. One convenient mutually <orthogonal> basis is
$$
b=(-\sqrt3,\sqrt3,0;-1,-1,2),\qquad d=(-\sqrt3,\sqrt3,0;1,1,-2),\qquad s=(-1,-1,2;-\sqrt3,\sqrt3,0).
$$
These vectors are also orthogonal to $t_x,t_y,r$, and each has squared length $12$. Their geometry comes from applying symmetric linear deformations to the equilibrium triangle: $b$ is uniform expansion, $d$ stretches one axis while compressing the other, and $s$ is the second independent shear pattern. Their extensions are
$$
\begin{array}{c|ccc}&e_{12}&e_{13}&e_{23}\\\hline
b&2\sqrt3&2\sqrt3&2\sqrt3\\
d&2\sqrt3&-\sqrt3&-\sqrt3\\
s&0&3&-3
\end{array}.
$$
Insert each row into the force equations. For example, $b$ gives $m\ddot x_1=3\sqrt3 k=-3k b_{x_1}$ and $m\ddot y_3=-6k=-3k b_{y_3}$, with the same proportionality in every component. The other two rows similarly give force $-3k/2$ times their displacement vector. Thus
$$
Kb=3kb,\qquad Kd=\tfrac32kd,\qquad Ks=\tfrac32ks,
$$
and the remaining <normal modes> and <normal frequencies> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}\text{pattern}&\omega^2&\omega\\\hline
b&3k/m&\sqrt{3k/m}\\
d&3k/(2m)&\sqrt{3k/(2m)}\\
s&3k/(2m)&\sqrt{3k/(2m)}
\end{array}.}
$$
Any orthogonal pair spanning the same two-dimensional shear subspace is equally valid, because its <eigenvalue> is repeated. The six displayed vectors are nonzero and mutually orthogonal, so they supply all six independent <normal modes> without solving a characteristic polynomial.

\Image[paper-2-triangle-normal-modes.png]
{title=Rotation, breathing and two shear patterns of the three-mass triangle}
{description=Arrows show infinitesimal displacements using the equilibrium coordinates in the paper. Translations supply two further zero modes. The breathing frequency squared is 3k/m; both shear frequencies squared are 3k/(2m).}

For explicit <normal coordinates>, normalize $t_x,t_y$ by $\sqrt3$ and $r,b,d,s$ by $\sqrt{12}$, and write $q=\sum_jQ_j\widehat v_j$. Since all masses are equal,
$$
T=\frac m2\sum_j\dot Q_j^2,\qquad V=\frac m2\sum_j\omega_j^2Q_j^2,\qquad\ddot Q_j+\omega_j^2Q_j=0.
$$
Positive-frequency coordinates have $Q_j=A_j\cos(\omega_jt)+B_j\sin(\omega_jt)$; the three rigid coordinates have the linear form found above.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

**Cosets and counting.** A <left coset> of a <subgroup> $H$ in a <group> $G$ is $gH=\{gh:h\in H\}$. The <left cosets> partition $G$ and each has $|H|$ elements. <Lagrange's theorem> states that for a finite <group>, $\boxed{|G|=[G:H]|H|}$; in particular, the <subgroup> order divides the <group order>.

**The matrix group.** Work throughout in the <finite field> $\mathbb F_5$, so every nonzero entry has a multiplicative inverse. Denote a matrix by $A(a,b,c)$, with $a,c\ne0$. Ordinary <matrix multiplication>, interpreted using <modular arithmetic>, gives
$$
A(a,b,c)A(d,e,f)=A(ad,ae+bf,cf).
$$
Its diagonal entries remain nonzero, proving closure. Associativity is inherited from <matrix multiplication>, $A(1,0,1)$ is the <identity element>, and
$$
\boxed{A(a,b,c)^{-1}=A(a^{-1},-a^{-1}bc^{-1},c^{-1}).}
$$
This lies in the same set and multiplication checks the inverse in either order. Thus these matrices form a <group>. There are four choices each for $a$ and $c$ and five for $b$, giving $\boxed{|G|=4\cdot5\cdot4=80}$.

**The subgroup.** Write $h(a,c)=A(a,a-c,c)$. Then
$$
h(a,c)h(d,f)=A(ad,a(d-f)+(a-c)f,cf)=h(ad,cf).
$$
It contains the identity $h(1,1)$, is closed, and has inverse $h(a^{-1},c^{-1})$. Interchanging the two factors leaves the product unchanged because multiplication in $\mathbb F_5$ is commutative. Therefore $H$ is an <abelian subgroup>, with
$$
\boxed{|H|=4^2=16,\qquad [G:H]=80/16=5.}
$$
Equivalently $H$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb F_5^\times\times\mathbb F_5^\times$ by $h(a,c)\mapsto(a,c)$, which also explains its abelian property. Its order divides $80$, verifying <Lagrange's theorem>.

There are five distinct <left cosets>. Representatives can be taken as $U_t=A(1,t,1)$, $t\in\mathbb F_5$: indeed
$$
U_th(a,c)=A(a,a-c+tc,c),\qquad t=(b-a+c)c^{-1}.
$$
Every matrix lies in exactly the coset specified by this unique $t$.

**Elements whose square is the identity.** Squaring gives
$$
A(a,b,c)^2=A(a^2,b(a+c),c^2).
$$
Thus $a,c\in\{1,-1\}=\{1,4\}$ and $b(a+c)=0$. For equal signs, $a+c\ne0$ in $\mathbb F_5$, so $b=0$. For opposite signs, $a+c=0$ and all five $b$ values work. The complete set is therefore
$$
\boxed{\{I,-I\}\ \cup\ \left\{\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}:b\in\mathbb F_5\right\}\ \cup\ \left\{\begin{pmatrix}-1&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}:b\in\mathbb F_5\right\}.}
$$
It contains $2+5+5=12$ elements, including the identity; the other eleven are <involutions>. Since $12$ does not divide $80$, <Lagrange's theorem> proves that this set is not a <subgroup>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

**Conjugacy classes.** For $a\in G$, its <conjugacy class> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(a)=\{gag^{-1}:g\in G\}.}
$$
Every element belongs to its own <conjugacy class>, by taking $g=e$. To prove uniqueness, suppose $x=gag^{-1}=hbh^{-1}$ belongs to both $\operatorname{Cl}(a)$ and $\operatorname{Cl}(b)$. Then
$$
b=(h^{-1}g)a(h^{-1}g)^{-1}.
$$
For every $t\in G$, this makes $tbt^{-1}$ a conjugate of $a$ as well. Hence $\operatorname{Cl}(b)\subseteq\operatorname{Cl}(a)$, and reversing the argument gives the other inclusion. Two <conjugacy classes> that meet are therefore equal, proving that the classes partition $G$.

**Abelian case.** If $G$ is an <abelian group>, then $gag^{-1}=agg^{-1}=a$ for every $g$, so $\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(a)=\{a\}}$ for each $a$.

**Centre.** An element has a singleton <conjugacy class> precisely when $gzg^{-1}=z$ for every $g$, equivalently $gz=zg$ for every $g$. Thus the definition in the paper agrees with the usual <centre of a group>:
$$
Z(G)=\{z\in G:gz=zg\text{ for every }g\in G\}.
$$
The identity lies in $Z(G)$. If $z,w\in Z(G)$, then for any $g$, $gzw=zgw=zwg$, so $zw\in Z(G)$. Also $gz=zg$ implies $z^{-1}g=gz^{-1}$, so $z^{-1}\in Z(G)$. This proves it is a <subgroup>. Any two of its elements commute because either one commutes with every element of $G$. Consequently $\boxed{Z(G)\text{ is an abelian subgroup of }G}$.

**Square symmetry.** Use the paper's notation $D_4$ for the eight-element <dihedral group> of a square. Let $r$ be a quarter-turn and $s$ a reflection, so $r^4=s^2=e$ and $srs=r^{-1}$. A rotation $r^j$ commutes with $s$ exactly when $r^j=r^{-j}$, or $2j=0$ modulo four. This selects $e$ and $r^2$. No reflection $r^js$ commutes with $r$, because
$$
(r^js)r=r^{j-1}s,\qquad r(r^js)=r^{j+1}s,
$$
and $r^{j-1}\ne r^{j+1}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{Z(D_4)=\{e,r^2\}=\{\text{identity},\text{half-turn}\}.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

**Definitions and labels.** A complex <group representation> on a vector space $V$ is a <group homomorphism> $\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V)$, so $\rho(gh)=\rho(g)\rho(h)$ and $\rho(e)=I$. It is an <irreducible representation> if $V\ne0$ and its only invariant subspaces are $0$ and $V$.

Read permutation matrices by their action on column basis vectors. The labels in the original PDF are
$$
I=e,\quad A=(123),\quad B=(132),\quad C=(13),\quad D=(12),\quad E=(23).
$$
In particular, the original PDF gives the last row of $C$ as $(1,0,0)$; the provided TeX transcription's $(0,0,1)$ is incorrect and would not give an invertible permutation matrix.

**Multiplication and conjugacy.** Matrix products act on columns with the right factor first. For example $A^2=B$, $AD=C$, and $DA=E$. The complete <multiplication table>, with row factor multiplied by column factor, is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}
 &I&A&B&C&D&E\\\hline
I&I&A&B&C&D&E\\
A&A&B&I&E&C&D\\
B&B&I&A&D&E&C\\
C&C&D&E&I&A&B\\
D&D&E&C&B&I&A\\
E&E&C&D&A&B&I
\end{array}.}
$$
Conjugating a <permutation> relabels its cycles. Thus the <conjugacy classes> are
$$
\boxed{\{I\},\qquad\{A,B\},\qquad\{C,D,E\},}
$$
of sizes one, two and three. These are respectively the identity, the three-cycles and the transpositions in the <symmetric group on three letters>.

**Dimensions.** The <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions> equals the <group order>, which here is six. A three-dimensional <irreducible representation> would already contribute nine, so the displayed representation is reducible. There are three inequivalent complex <irreducible representations>, one for each <conjugacy class>, including the <trivial representation> of dimension one. If their dimensions are $1,d_2,d_3$, then $d_2^2+d_3^2=5$, whose positive integer solution is $\{d_2,d_3\}=\{1,2\}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{irreducible dimensions: }1,1,2.}
$$
One can also see reducibility directly: every permutation matrix fixes $(1,1,1)^T$.

**Characters.** The <character of a representation> is the <trace> of its representing matrix, constant on each <conjugacy class>. The given <permutation representation> has character equal to the number of fixed basis vectors:
$$
\boxed{\chi_{\rm perm}=(3,0,1)}
$$
in class order $(I;A,B;C,D,E)$. A <trivial representation> has character $(1,1,1)$. For the second one-dimensional character, a transposition has value $\pm1$ because its square is the identity. The two three-cycles are conjugate and inverse, so their common value $a$ obeys both $a^2=1$ and $a^3=1$, forcing $a=1$. <Character orthogonality> with the trivial character then requires $1+2+3b=0$, giving $b=-1$. This determines the <sign representation> without constructing its matrices.

Write the two-dimensional character as $(2,x,y)$. <Character orthogonality> with the first two rows gives
$$
2+2x+3y=0,\qquad2+2x-3y=0,
$$
so $x=-1$, $y=0$. Its squared norm is $(4+2)/6=1$, as required. The complete <character table> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrr}
\text{class}&I&A,B&C,D,E\\\hline
\text{class size}&1&2&3\\\hline
\chi_{\rm triv}&1&1&1\\
\chi_{\rm sign}&1&1&-1\\
\chi_{\rm std}&2&-1&0
\end{array}.}
$$
The last row belongs to the <standard representation of the symmetric group>.

To decompose the given representation, use the <character inner product>
$$
\langle\chi,\psi\rangle=\frac16\left[\chi(I)\overline{\psi(I)}+2\chi(A)\overline{\psi(A)}+3\chi(C)\overline{\psi(C)}\right].
$$
Its multiplicities are
$$
\langle\chi_{\rm perm},\chi_{\rm triv}\rangle=\frac{3+3}{6}=1,\qquad
\langle\chi_{\rm perm},\chi_{\rm sign}\rangle=\frac{3-3}{6}=0,\qquad
\langle\chi_{\rm perm},\chi_{\rm std}\rangle=\frac6{6}=1.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\rho_{\rm perm}\cong\mathbf1_{\rm triv}\oplus\rho_{\rm std},\qquad3=1+2.}
$$
Concretely the invariant line is spanned by $(1,1,1)^T$ and its invariant complement is $\{(x,y,z):x+y+z=0\}$. Their characters are exactly the first and third rows, agreeing with the decomposition obtained entirely from <character orthogonality>.
